1
00:00:02,400 --> 00:00:05,081
Meet the plant manager in Korea.

2
00:00:05,081 --> 00:00:10,362
He signs in, switches to English, and opens his hiring requests.

3
00:00:10,362 --> 00:00:13,693
For a new role, he skips the long form —

4
00:00:13,693 --> 00:00:17,918
InterviewPath records a short voice intake instead,

5
00:00:17,918 --> 00:00:22,224
so his own words become the brief HR will work from.

6
00:00:22,524 --> 00:00:24,370
Now the candidate's side.

7
00:00:24,370 --> 00:00:28,771
She finds the open Backend Developer posting, uploads her CV,

8
00:00:28,771 --> 00:00:31,398
and applies — no account needed yet.

9
00:00:31,398 --> 00:00:35,799
In seconds, InterviewPath's AI has read her CV and shows her,

10
00:00:35,799 --> 00:00:40,272
requirement by requirement, what it already sees evidence for.

11
00:00:40,272 --> 00:00:45,241
Being a strong match, she's invited straight to a spoken AI interview

12
00:00:45,241 --> 00:00:47,158
instead of a written test.

13
00:00:47,158 --> 00:00:50,281
This room is just waiting for a microphone,

14
00:00:50,281 --> 00:00:52,908
same as the manager's intake screen.

15
00:00:53,208 --> 00:00:58,333
Meanwhile at HR: the evidence board for a different applicant to the

16
00:00:58,333 --> 00:00:59,150
same role.

17
00:00:59,150 --> 00:01:02,344
Pham Thi Lan sits in the Hidden Gem lane —

18
00:01:02,344 --> 00:01:07,767
her CV alone didn't meet the requirements, but her spoken interview did.

19
00:01:07,767 --> 00:01:12,669
A plain CV filter would have rejected her before anyone heard her

20
00:01:12,669 --> 00:01:14,080
answer a question.

21
00:01:14,080 --> 00:01:18,240
HR reads the evidence and approves her to move forward.

22
00:01:18,540 --> 00:01:21,514
Approving isn't the end of HR's job —

23
00:01:21,514 --> 00:01:26,602
it's translating evidence into something the manager can act on.

24
00:01:26,602 --> 00:01:31,925
One click drafts a brief from the real interview quotes, in Korean.

25
00:01:31,925 --> 00:01:35,134
HR reads it, marks the wording reviewed,

26
00:01:35,134 --> 00:01:37,561
and only then can it be sent —

27
00:01:37,561 --> 00:01:40,692
the server refuses anything unreviewed.

28
00:01:40,992 --> 00:01:42,623
Back to the manager.

29
00:01:42,623 --> 00:01:46,507
HR's brief now waits in his own interviews list —

30
00:01:46,507 --> 00:01:50,779
the candidate's quotes, in his language, no CV jargon.

31
00:01:50,779 --> 00:01:56,527
He reads it, scores each requirement, and submits his own recommendation.

32
00:01:56,527 --> 00:02:01,576
HR still makes the final call, but his voice shaped the question

33
00:02:01,576 --> 00:02:03,984
and now shapes the answer too.

34
00:02:04,284 --> 00:02:05,473
Last stop: HR.

35
00:02:05,473 --> 00:02:10,863
With the manager's recommendation in hand, HR records the outcome —

36
00:02:10,863 --> 00:02:15,065
hired — and starts the onboarding plan in one click.

37
00:02:15,065 --> 00:02:20,376
A candidate a CV filter would have screened out just became Daehan

38
00:02:20,376 --> 00:02:26,004
Precision's newest engineer, and the whole trail — manager, candidate,

39
00:02:26,004 --> 00:02:28,620
HR — stays attached to her file.

